  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft is primarily zonal with a weak upper-\nlevel high pressure system centered over Colorado. This zonal flow \nis held by ensembles for a week until at least early next week with \ntroughing near the west coast. That system appears at this point to \nbe the only chance for >5% precipitation until it arrives. In the \nmeantime, a very consistent pattern of seasonably warm and dry \nweather across SW Kansas. \n\nLows tonight are forecast to be in the mid-to-upper 50s. Winds in \nthe overnight will stay mostly light tonight with the southwest \nwinds shifting to out of the northeast around 16Z as a subtle \nboundary crawls across the CWA. The HRRR has fog developing around 4-\n7 AM around Garden City. It is interesting that this only is present \nin the very localized area, but fog development can not be ruled out \nespecially in fog-prone areas to night. Occasionally fog can be \nunderrepresented in models in marginal setups, so it will need to be \nmonitored into the night and early morning. If fog does develop, \ntake proper travel precautions. Any fog should clear quickly after \nsunrise. \n\nTomorrow is forecast to see highs in the mid-80s. This is around 10 \ndegrees warmer than average for this time of year across SW Kansas. \nHighs of around the mid-80s will be present all week until at least \nMonday next week. Tuesday night will be in a similar fog environment \nas tonight, so if fog is able to develop tonight, the expectation is \nthat more fog will be present tomorrow night. Beyond the fog, no \nactive weather or weather headlines are forecast until at least \nearly next week with the pattern change. The weekend is forecast to \nhave breezy winds of 20-25 mph out of the south, but not abnormal \nfor SW Kansas. Relative humidities should stay high enough to \nprevent much of an increased fire weather risk. However, as always \ntake precautions with ignition sources especially as the vegetation \ndries out.",
